<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1358">1358</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Ilyas Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilyas-shahi-dynasty">Ilyas Shahi dynasty</a> and of the independent Bengal Sultanate. Ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a>, he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satgaon">Satgaon</a> and the Sonargaon Sultanate and in 1352 united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> as a single state, taking the title Shah-i-Bangalah. He withstood a siege at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekdala">Ekdala</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-tughluq">Firuz Shah Tughluq</a> in 1353, securing Bengal's independence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ilyas Shah was a Sunni Muslim whose origins lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> region, in what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, though whether he was born there is disputed. According to Syed A M R Haque, his forebears came to the subcontinent as Muslim missionaries and were granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagirs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> in 1227. He rose through the Delhi Sultanate's service, by one account first under Malik Firuz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and later under Izzuddin Yahya, an imperial provincial governor in Bengal. In the early 14th century the sultanate ruled Bengal as three provinces, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satgaon">Satgaon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonargaon">Sonargaon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Yahya's death in 1338 Ilyas took the title Shams ad-Din and fought a long war, from 1339 to 1352, against Sultan Alauddin Ali Shah and Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikhtiyaruddin-ghazi-shah">Ikhtiyaruddin Ghazi Shah</a>. Having united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, he moved his capital to Pandua, once a seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> kings and Pala emperors. He kept an army of 90,000 cavalry and many elephants. In 1346 he led the first Muslim army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirhut">Tirhut</a> and raiding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, where his troops sacked the temple of Swayambhunath and looted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> for three days. He also invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajpur">Jajpur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a>, and was the first Muslim king to capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-tughluq">Firuz Shah Tughluq</a> invaded in November 1353 and occupied Pandua, Ilyas held out in the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekdala">Ekdala</a> for two months, then pursued the withdrawing army as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>. Firuz Shah returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1355, and Ilyas's realm reached the Koshi River. His coins call him the second Alexander. Persian became his court language, and later in his reign he exchanged gifts with Firuz Shah, sending elephants for Turkish and Arabian horses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ilyas Shah died in January 1358 and was buried in Hajipur, the former village of Ukkacala, which he had fortified and which was renamed in his honour. In 1587 Makhsus Shah, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, built a congregational mosque inside his fort there. A mazar on SDO Road attributed to him is revered by Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, who hold a mela at his Urus each year. His dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> for 145 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah</b>, who seized power at Lakhnauti in the early 1340s, took Satgaon and then Sonargaon, and by 1352 had united the whole delta under one ruler for the first time.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1352 <b>Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah</b> of Lakhnauti conquered Sonargaon, uniting all of Bengal under the Bengal Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/sonargaon-sultanate">Sonargaon Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in Bengal, in what is now <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31,62&amp;year=1348" aria-label="Sistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6153 1034 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6603 1304h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6603 1304h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31,62&amp;year=1348">What was here in 1348? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
